WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.066
Use-after-free-like defects are not limited to heap deletion.

2
00:00:04.066 --> 00:00:08.132
Containers can invalidate iterators, references, pointers, and views into their storage.

3
00:00:08.132 --> 00:00:16.633
After invalidation, the old access may still look like a normal value, but it no longer refers to a valid element or buffer.

4
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:19.960
That makes container mutation a common lifetime review point.

5
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.808
Reallocation is a familiar example.

6
00:00:21.808 --> 00:00:29.570
Growing a std::vector or std::string may move elements to new storage, invalidating pointers and references into the old buffer.

7
00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:37.332
Erase operations can invalidate iterators and references to erased elements, and sometimes to elements after the erased position depending on the

8
00:00:37.332 --> 00:00:37.702
container.

9
00:00:37.702 --> 00:00:42.137
Resizing can destroy elements, change buffers, or alter assumptions about valid ranges.

10
00:00:42.137 --> 00:00:43.986
Views require the same care.

11
00:00:43.986 --> 00:00:48.791
A std::span or std::string_view does not own the data it describes.

12
00:00:48.791 --> 00:00:56.923
If the source container, string, or array is destroyed or modified in a way that invalidates storage, the view can become stale.

13
00:00:56.923 --> 00:01:03.576
A view that is stored in an object or returned from a function should trigger a lifetime review.

14
00:01:03.576 --> 00:01:09.120
Different containers provide different stability guarantees, so avoid carrying assumptions from one type to another.

15
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:17.622
A node-based container may keep some references stable across operations that would invalidate a vector reference, but erased elements are still gone.

16
00:01:17.622 --> 00:01:22.796
A deque, map, vector, string, and unordered container each have their own invalidation rules.

17
00:01:22.796 --> 00:01:27.232
The secure habit is to check the rule at the mutation point.

18
00:01:27.232 --> 00:01:30.559
Prevention starts by keeping access and mutation close together.

19
00:01:30.559 --> 00:01:33.146
Avoid long-lived references into mutable containers.

20
00:01:33.146 --> 00:01:38.690
Prefer indexes or stable identifiers when appropriate, and revalidate after operations that may invalidate access.

21
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:42.387
Document invalidation assumptions when an API returns views or iterators.

22
00:01:42.387 --> 00:01:49.040
In code review, ask what happens to every outstanding pointer, reference, iterator, or view after each container mutation.
